      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into a zoning issue in Austin. We have a client trying to reclassify a parcel from R-1 (single-family residential) to a mixed-use designation, but we've run into some conflicting language in the municipal code. Specifically, there's a discrepancy between the setback requirements for new builds and the definitions regarding existing encroachments from adjacent properties. How should we approach reconciling these conflicting clauses to argue for the reclassification?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "First, thoroughly review the municipal code to gather all relevant sections that pertain to R-1 and mixed-use areas, focusing on setback requirements and encroachment definitions. Seek out any recent updates or interpretations that might clarify the discrepancies. Compiling case studies or precedents where similar zoning issues were resolved can be valuable in supporting your argument. Approaching the zoning board with an organized presentation that highlights the benefits of allowing mixed-use in the area, while also addressing the concerns raised by the conflicting language, will strengthen your case. Lastly, consider consulting a local zoning expert or attorney to ensure your argument meets all legal requirements and boosts your chances of success.",
